From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Ludovic Henry <git@ludovic.dev>
Date: Mon, 5 Oct 2026 00:00:00 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] build_config: add a riscv64 target

CURRENT_PLATFORM's Linux/64-bit branch only checks machine() == "aarch64"
before falling back to LINUX_64 for everything else, so on riscv64 it
silently selects LINUX_64 and both OpenSSL builds Configure with
"linux-x86_64" - the wrong target, picked only because riscv64 was never
considered rather than because it was ruled out.

Add a LINUX_RISCV64 platform and route it to each OpenSSL build's own
target:

- ModernOpenSslBuildConfig (pinned to OpenSSL_1_1_1w) uses
  "linux64-riscv64", a real Configure target on that tag. It carries no
  riscv64-specific assembly in this OpenSSL release (RISC-V crypto asm
  landed in the 3.x series, well after 1.1.1 went EOL) - verified there is
  no crypto/*/asm/*riscv64* source anywhere in the OpenSSL_1_1_1w tree -
  so the build is effectively the same portable C codegen as any other
  target here, without the relocation-range asm pitfalls newer OpenSSL
  riscv64 asm can hit in a large shared object (see CLAUDE.md gotcha 625
  in the RISE python-wheels repo, hit by a different, newer-OpenSSL
  package).
- LegacyOpenSslBuildConfig (pinned to OpenSSL_1_0_2e) predates riscv64
  entirely - that tag's Configure has no riscv* target at all - so it
  falls back to "linux-generic64" the same way this class already special
  -cases OSX_ARM64 to "linux-aarch64" two lines above. _OPENSSL_CONF_CMD
  already hardcodes "no-asm" for this legacy build on every platform, so
  the generic target's C fallback is exactly what was going to be built
  anyway.

Also add LINUX_RISCV64 to all_linux_platforms(), so setup.py's
-Wl,--exclude-libs=ALL (hiding the statically-linked OpenSSL symbols to
avoid colliding with a system libssl/libcrypto the interpreter may also
load, see upstream issue #95) applies on riscv64 too, the same as on
every other Linux architecture nassl supports.

Upstream-Status: To upstream [not yet submitted; riscv64 is not part of nabla-c0d3/nassl's own CI matrix, so nothing depends on it, but the fix is small, has no RISE-specific assumptions, and is generally applicable]

Signed-off-by: Ludovic Henry <git@ludovic.dev>
---
 build_config.py | 12 ++++++++++++
 1 file changed, 12 insertions(+)

diff --git a/build_config.py b/build_config.py
index 4cf8af5..41da25f 100644
--- a/build_config.py
+++ b/build_config.py
@@ -30,6 +30,7 @@ class SupportedPlatformEnum(Enum):
     OSX_ARM64 = 7
     LINUX_ARM64 = 8
     LINUX_ARM32 = 9
+    LINUX_RISCV64 = 10

     @classmethod
     def all_linux_platforms(cls) -> List["SupportedPlatformEnum"]:
@@ -38,6 +39,7 @@ class SupportedPlatformEnum(Enum):
             cls.LINUX_64,
             cls.LINUX_ARM32,
             cls.LINUX_ARM64,
+            cls.LINUX_RISCV64,
         ]


@@ -51,6 +53,8 @@ if architecture()[0] == "64bit":
     elif platform in ["linux", "linux2"]:
         if machine() == "aarch64":
             CURRENT_PLATFORM = SupportedPlatformEnum.LINUX_ARM64
+        elif machine() == "riscv64":
+            CURRENT_PLATFORM = SupportedPlatformEnum.LINUX_RISCV64
         else:
             CURRENT_PLATFORM = SupportedPlatformEnum.LINUX_64
     elif platform == "win32":
@@ -158,6 +162,8 @@ class OpenSslBuildConfig(BuildConfig, ABC):
             openssl_target = "linux-aarch64"
         elif self.platform == SupportedPlatformEnum.LINUX_ARM32:
             openssl_target = "linux-armv4"
+        elif self.platform == SupportedPlatformEnum.LINUX_RISCV64:
+            openssl_target = "linux64-riscv64"
         else:
             raise ValueError("Unknown platform")

@@ -263,6 +269,12 @@ class LegacyOpenSslBuildConfig(OpenSslBuildConfig):
         if self.platform == SupportedPlatformEnum.OSX_ARM64:
             # Note there's no debug build variant available here
             return "linux-aarch64"
+        if self.platform == SupportedPlatformEnum.LINUX_RISCV64:
+            # OpenSSL 1.0.2e (this legacy build's pinned tag) predates riscv64 support;
+            # it has no "linux64-riscv64" Configure target at all. Fall back to the
+            # generic 64-bit target, which the base _OPENSSL_CONF_CMD above already
+            # builds with "no-asm" regardless of target.
+            return "linux-generic64"
         return super()._get_build_target(should_build_for_debug)

     @property
--
2.43.0
